※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4554

※ A capture list says what a lambda depends on, so a name in it the body never
※ reaches is a thing the list says and the lambda does not do.  It costs room
※ in what the lambda carries and a copy where it is written, and -- worse than
※ either -- it tells a reader the lambda depends on something it does not.
※
※ Reading it is using it and so is writing it: a name brought in by reference
※ may be brought in *to* be written, which is the whole of what `&` is for.
※
※ `[=]` and `[&]` bring in what the body reaches, so they cannot bring in too
※ much; the rule is asked of them all the same, and where it ever fires of one
※ it is the compiler that has got it wrong and not the program.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: u8 = 1u8
    let m: u8 = 2u8
    let f: fn(u8) → u8 = λ a: u8 [n, m] → u8 { a + n }
    if f(1u8) ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
